Through the harness after the fix, --test pulse_virus --duration 30 at the default --monitor-period 1:
the row reports sample_period_s 2.0, i.e. monitor_period_for asked for one whole cycle.

    === --test pulse_virus --duration 30
    [PANTHEON-NEURON] 1 device(s) [trn1], 1 workload(s)
    [PANTHEON-NEURON] cores 0 to the workload, 1 reserved for neuron-profile
    [PANTHEON-NEURON] -> pulse_virus
    [PANTHEON-NEURON]    PASS
    [PANTHEON-NEURON]    82.53% of 47.5 TFLOPS across 1 of 2 core(s)
    [PANTHEON-NEURON] report: /home/ubuntu/pn/database/pantheon_neuron_report_20260911_211805.json
      pulse_virus: PASS Score=39.2022 method=neuron-monitor repeats=None period=2.0
    whole periods 13 of 15, mean 39.202, all-sample mean 38.372, highest period 39.59 TFLOPS
    kernel over the 30 s window: 39.802 TFLOPS (observed duty 0.5183) -> monitor -1.5%

Why -1.5% here and -0.03% in the probe: the measured cycle is ~2.07 s, not 2, so a 2 s
window slides against the pulse and its loaded fraction drifts with phase. Over 13 periods
the phase moves under half a cycle, so the bias does not average out fully. It is bounded
and never produces a zero; at 1 s the same run shape read 3-4% low with periods from 14 to 68.
